- time: 00:55:37
  seconds: 3337
  topic: Superintendent's Report - Federal Title Funding/ESEA (Angela Atkinson Duina)
  desc: Superintendent Atkinson Duina introduced Dr. Prince to provide an update on
    federal funding for the upcoming budget year, specifically focusing on the Elementary
    and Secondary Education Act (ESEA) and Every Student Succeeds Act (ESSA) funds.
  children:
  - seconds: 3395
    speaker: Dr. Prince
    text: 'Dr. Prince, Assistant Superintendent, explained that the district receives
      four types of federal title funds: Title I for lower socioeconomic students,
      Title II-A for professional development, Title III for multilingual students,
      and Title IV for student support and academic enrichment. She noted that funds
      can be combined for projects.'
  - seconds: 3485
    speaker: Dr. Prince
    text: Dr. Prince reported a significant decrease of almost $150,000 in federal
      funding for South Portland, primarily in Title I, due to a lower state allocation
      and new guidance for charter school student allocations. She highlighted that
      Title IV has remained the most steady over the past three years.
  - seconds: 3565
    speaker: Dr. Prince
    text: Dr. Prince explained that carry-forward funds from FY24 will help mitigate
      the FY25 decrease, preventing an additional request from the board this year.
      However, she stressed the importance of considering this reduction for the FY26
      budget to maintain support for Skillin and Kaler schools.
  - seconds: 3665
    speaker: Dr. Prince
    text: Dr. Prince noted that the district acts as a fiscal agent for three non-public
      schools (Holy Cross, Greater Portland Christian Academy, Portland Kids Academy),
      which also receive federal allocations. These funds are held by the district
      and used to purchase items or pay contractors on behalf of these schools.
  - seconds: 3725
    speaker: Dr. Prince
    text: Dr. Prince outlined the proposed use of FY25 funds, with 65% allocated to
      Skillin and the rest to Kaler for math literacy and intervention roles, as they
      are school-wide receivers. Funds will also support a regular education social
      work position and a behavior strategist across elementary schools.
  - seconds: 3815
    speaker: Dr. Prince
    text: Dr. Prince detailed that $65,000 will support multilingual students through
      supplemental programming, including multilingual specialists, professional development,
      and family engagement initiatives. Additionally, $25,000 from Title II will
      be allocated for professional learning.
