- time: 01:15:35
  seconds: 4535
  topic: Board Discussion on Facilities Bond Referendum
  desc: Board members discussed the proposed bond referendum, particularly the artificial
    turf component. Concerns were raised about the true recyclability of plastic products
    and the environmental impact, while others emphasized the need for durable, accessible
    facilities for students.
  children:
  - seconds: 4545
    speaker: Claire Holman
    text: Board member Claire Holman expressed skepticism about the recyclability
      claims of artificial turf, calling plastic recycling 'greenwashing.' She questioned
      the cost and logistics of recycling the turf and infill, advocating for natural
      turf as a more environmentally responsible choice, aligning with the 'one climate
      future' goal.
  - seconds: 4705
    speaker: Ms. DeAngelis
    text: Board Chair Rosemarie DeAngelis acknowledged similar questions and concerns
      regarding artificial turf, particularly its environmental impact. She stated
      that while she understands the arguments for artificial turf, she would need
      more thorough information and consideration of these issues before making a
      decision.
  - seconds: 4810
    speaker: Angela Atkinson Duina
    text: Superintendent Atkinson Duina presented cost comparisons, noting that a
      complete overhaul of the natural grass field would cost around $10.5-12.3 million
      due to current poor conditions. He offered to provide more information on the
      recycling process and costs associated with Iron Turf.
  - seconds: 4860
    speaker: Claire Holman
    text: Board member Claire Holman cited the NFL Players Association's call for
      eliminating artificial turf due to injury concerns, suggesting that schools
      are being offered 'reject' products. She also raised concerns about artificial
      turf harboring bacteria and being less sanitary than natural grass.
  - seconds: 4955
    speaker: Elliot Wertheimer
    text: Student Representative Elliot Wertheimer acknowledged the problems with
      artificial turf but argued that comparing a school district to the NFL, which
      has billions to maintain natural fields, is not equitable. He suggested that
      artificial turf offers a more cost-effective solution for extended use.
  - seconds: 5015
    speaker: Ms. Kinney
    text: Board member Ms. Kinney asked about the expected lifetime of the proposed
      turf field. Steve Chris Foley from Activ tos, a consultant, responded that turf
      companies typically offer an eight-year warranty, but Iron Turf's durable fiber
      could extend its life to 10-12 years or more, with only the carpet needing replacement.
  - seconds: 5175
    speaker: Ms. Kinney
    text: Board member Ms. Kinney questioned if the current field's poor condition
      was due to a lack of proper maintenance. Steve Chris Foley from Activ tos explained
      that few communities have the budget to maintain a natural grass field properly
      for safety, leading to limited usability and frequent closures.
  - seconds: 5285
    speaker: Claire Holman
    text: Board member Claire Holman reiterated her concerns about the environmental
      impact, stating that the city's 'one climate future' goals should prioritize
      sustainability over convenience or immediate benefits. She emphasized the need
      for more detailed documentation on recyclability and alignment with environmental
      objectives.
  - seconds: 5391
    speaker: Ms. Richardson
    text: Board member Eleni Richardson expressed her comfort with supporting Iron
      Turf after researching its properties. She highlighted that Iron Turf's resin
      is already recycled and, due to its woven structure without polyurethane, can
      be completely melted down and reused for new fields, aligning with climate goals.
